    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "dc1f7529_76508a01",
        "filename": "src/core/SkBlendModeBlender.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 21
      },
      "lineNbr": 71,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-10-25T14:57:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The way we control for not knowing the input color is by connecting the blocks and their children in the right tree-structure. For example, a color filter block doesn\u0027t inherently know its input color either. Today, the color filter block comes immediately after the shader block, and it uses \"prior stage output\" to grab the color from that. However, as you know, we want to redesign this structure anyway--in the new design, the shader block would be a _child_ of the color filter block.\n\nThe only place where we need special treatment is in the final blend against the destination surface. We need to use fixed-function hardware or do readback on the color attachment. But AFAICS that is a totally separate case? Primitive blenders are not the special case--blending against the destination surface is.",
      "parentUuid": "86e2cea7_77273a75",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 71,
        "startChar": 29,
        "endLine": 71,
        "endChar": 55
      },
      "revId": "8319057f813f4a5d0dfbb0357cce5d0cc8b55709",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "903f17db_2f36deff",
        "filename": "src/core/SkBlendModeBlender.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 21
      },
      "lineNbr": 71,
      "author": {
        "id": 18914
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-10-27T17:28:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I see what you mean in regards to primitive blending not being the special case since they do not require dest reads. In previous comment chains, we had talked about having different type of blending blocks, but I suppose I did not extrapolate that to mean four different blend \"types\". Can you explicate what you mean by types? Do you mean snippets, classes? Besides having the different blocks, I\u0027m not sure what advantage splitting this up further would grant us.\n\nI understand the color filter example - did you envision having an `SkPrimitiveBlender`(Or a more inclusive name to represent cases of blending without dest reads; suggestions welcome) that is totally separate from `SkBlender`? That can be done, I just want to be sure I understand why that would be beneficial as opposed to just leveraging the existing `SkBlender` class to choose between creating one type of blend block versus another, and then decide whether to perform the dest read or not within SkSL (opting to do so when blending against the surface; opting not to when doing primitive blending/no surface read).",
      "parentUuid": "dc1f7529_76508a01",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 71,
        "startChar": 29,
        "endLine": 71,
        "endChar": 55
      },
      "revId": "8319057f813f4a5d0dfbb0357cce5d0cc8b55709",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2806e36a_6c45652c",
        "filename": "src/core/SkBlendModeBlender.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 21
      },
      "lineNbr": 71,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-10-27T19:09:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\"Did you envision having an SkPrimitiveBlender(Or a more inclusive name to represent cases of blending without dest reads; suggestions welcome) that is totally separate from SkBlender?\"\n\nNo, that\u0027s not what I\u0027m trying to suggest at all. Sorry if my description wasn\u0027t clear :( \n\nA SkBlender describes how to mix two colors together, it doesn\u0027t have anything to do with the origin of those colors. The blender doesn\u0027t need to know that the source color came from the primitive on a drawVertices call (or anywhere else). A blender can hypothetically be invoked anywhere in the shader tree; any runtime effect can evaluate a blend. All a blender needs to know is \"the source color is #AARRGGBB and the dest color is #AARRGGBB and the blend mode is this SkBlendMode\" (or, the blend is described by this SkSL function). And it then generates code that does that blend. Well, more exactly, it generates a Key that uniquely describes a Block that knows how to emit that code.\n\nThere\u0027s one special case, and that is when it\u0027s the very last step before we cover the destination surface. There\u0027s still a destination color in this case, but the difference is that we don\u0027t need to supply it in our program, the GPU supplies it by reading the pixel that we\u0027re drawing on top of. Handling this type of blend should be an implementation detail of the paint-param building process, where we detect that the blend uses a SkBlendMode and that this blend is occurring as the very last thing in the tree. When this is the case, we need to use a totally different Block (the BlendModeBlock) here. This type of Block only takes as inputs the source color and the blend mode; the destination color is supplied implicitly by the GPU and the blend is performed by dedicated GPU hardware. AFAICS this special case doesn\u0027t need to bleed into SkBlender or into onAddToKey at all. We handle it when we decide on which Blocks to emit. The final blend should just emit a different type of Key/Block entirely, one where there\u0027s no child slot for a destination color.",
      "parentUuid": "903f17db_2f36deff",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 71,
        "startChar": 29,
        "endLine": 71,
        "endChar": 55
      },
      "revId": "8319057f813f4a5d0dfbb0357cce5d0cc8b55709",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1e7e96d9_9d04a304",
        "filename": "src/core/SkBlendModeBlender.cpp",
